      "page_title": "Choosing the Right Tool",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Each Costs"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Lua: harder to debug and test, cache management, blocks server",
            "WATCH: retry loops, contention sensitivity, connection affinity",
            "Pipeline: O(N) commands in 1 RTT, zero guarantees"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Approach",
            "Complexity"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Pipeline",
              "N cmds, 1 RTT"
            ],
            [
              "WATCH loop",
              "O(N x retries) RTT"
            ],
            [
              "Lua",
              "1 RTT + O(work)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Modern Home",
          "text": "Redis 7 Functions are where anything you'd ship as a long-lived EVALSHA belongs."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Transaction Misreads",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Assuming MULTI rolls back on a runtime error — partial effects persist",
            "Ignoring the nil from EXEC after WATCH: a lost race, silently",
            "Reading inside MULTI and expecting a value — you get `+QUEUED`",
            "Using a connection pool with WATCH: WATCH and EXEC on different sockets"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Cluster Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Hardcoding key names in Lua instead of KEYS — `CROSSSLOT` in Cluster",
            "All keys in one MULTI or script must hash to a single slot",
            "Fix: hash tags — `{user:1}:name` and `{user:1}:age` share a slot"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "bash",
          "lines": [
            "MSET user:1:a 1 user:1:b 2   # CROSSSLOT",
            "MSET {user:1}:a 1 {user:1}:b 2  # one slot"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Script Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A long script stalls the whole server; `SCRIPT KILL` is useless post-write",
            "`KEYS *` inside a script: O(N) over the keyspace, everything blocked",
            "Relying on float truncation — returning 3.7 hands the client 3",
            "Returning a Lua table with a nil hole loses every element after it"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Lock Mistakes"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "Broken",
                "lines": [
                  "SETNX then EXPIRE",
                  "crash = leaked lock",
                  "DEL to release"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "Correct",
                "lines": [
                  "SET k tok NX EX 30",
                  "Lua compare-and-del",
                  "owner token checked"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A crash between `SETNX` and `EXPIRE` leaks the lock forever",
            "A bare `DEL` release can delete someone else's lock"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Common Mistakes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Durability Assumptions"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "EXEC is atomic in memory, not fsynced unless `appendfsync always`",
            "Replication is async — a failover can lose an acknowledged EXEC",
            "Pipelining a million commands in one flush OOMs the output buffer",
            "Watching a key a TTL will expire, then blaming Redis for the nil"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Mistake Pattern",
          "text": "Every item here is Redis doing exactly what it documents, and code assuming otherwise."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What Atomic Means Here",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "No interleaving of other clients' commands, single-threaded serial run",
            "NOT: rollback on error",
            "NOT: snapshot isolation across separate transactions",
            "NOT: any cross-key durability barrier"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Durability Is Orthogonal"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "Loss window"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "RDB snapshot",
              "recent writes"
            ],
            [
              "AOF everysec",
              "up to 1s"
            ],
            [
              "appendfsync always",
              "none, but slow"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A successful EXEC only means: ran in memory, on the primary",
            "Replication is async by default — failover can drop an acked EXEC",
            "`WAIT numreplicas timeout` improves the odds, is not consensus",
            "Cluster gives no cross-slot atomicity — plan hash tags up front"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Final Warning",
          "text": "Atomic is not durable, and single-node atomic is not distributed atomic."
        }
      ]
    },
